vim 9.1.0 (Vim)

programs/vim.wasm is built from the upstream source below, with the changes listed under it. Together they
are the complete corresponding source of the binary; this is the offer of it.

Upstream source
  vim-9.1.0.tar.gz  (vim 9.1.0, Vim)
    sha256 ddb435f6e386c53799a3025bdc5a3533beac735a0ee596cb27ada97366a1c725
    from https://github.com/vim/vim/archive/refs/tags/v9.1.0.tar.gz

Changes and build scripts (in wasm/ of the package, and in the repository under core/utils-copyleft/wasm/)
  core/utils/wasm/lib/ecmaos/termcap.c (in the repository; wasm/lib/ecmaos/termcap.c in the npm package @ecmaos/coreutils)
    linked into the program: a termcap that finds no terminal, so Vim uses its builtin terminal entries
  wasm/vim/build.sh
    configure flags and defines (SYS_VIMRC_FILE, VIM_DEFAULTS_FILE, USE_SYSTEM, MODIFIED_BY, and <sys/wait.h> included), and one line of src/ui.c changed: inchar_loop's wait for a key stops at zero instead of waiting forever when the time already passed is taken off a wait of none

The changes were made in 2026; the repository history dates each one.

Toolchain: WALI's musl and the LLVM runtimes (musl 243ec00c3ffb910375db487d7eb24ea76d1e2c9f, LLVM 22.1.8), with the patches in utils/wali/patches;
utils/wali-version in the repository pins them, and utils/wali/build-sysroot.sh builds them. Its compiler then
puts a signal check in each loop of the linked program (utils/wali/signal-checks.mjs).

Where to get it
  - /sources/vim-9.1.0.tar.gz on the site that served this program, and the other archives listed above in the same place
  - the sources/ and wasm/ directories of the npm package @ecmaos/coreutils-copyleft, in the version that holds this file
  - https://github.com/ecmaos/ecmaos (core/utils-copyleft), at the tag of the release that holds this file
